    <paragraph>The Laplace transform of a continuous function over the interval $$y = \frac{1}{\left( {x + 1} \right)^{3/2}},$$ is defined by $$x = 3.$$ (see the Student Project). This definition is used to solve some important initial-value problems in differential equations, as discussed later. The domain of F is the set of all real numbers s such that the improper integral converges. Find the Laplace transform F of each of the following functions and give the domain of F.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="401" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = \frac{5}{1 + x^{2}}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$1 s , s &gt; 0 1 s , s &gt; 0$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="402" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = \frac{3}{x}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="403" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$x = 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$s s 2 + 4 , s &gt; 0 s s 2 + 4 , s &gt; 0$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="404" has_solution="false">
